<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Feeding a household is the most relentless recurring project a family
    runs: seven dinners a week, a budget, somebody's allergy, and a fridge
    with three things in it. The knowledge to do it lives in a shoebox, six
    screenshot folders, and a phone call to your mother.
  </p>
  <p>
    The paid answer is a meal-kit subscription. The free one is a recipe
    site that buries four lines of method under nine hundred words of memoir
    and eleven adverts, and quietly owns the collection you paste into it.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("recipes") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it does</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Understands the ingredients") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Enough to scale the batch properly — cooking for nine when the
        recipe serves four, without you doing fractions at the counter with
        floury hands.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Books, not just a pile") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A soup book, a spring book, the one that's just grandma's. Curated
        by you, which is the part that makes a recipe collection feel like
        something rather than a folder.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Shared however far you like") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        To the household in a click, or outside it by a link that works
        without an account. Send someone the book rather than screenshots.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Portable on purpose") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A shared link can also be read by another copy of this app, which
        can simply take the recipe in. Passing food between households
        shouldn't require either of you to sign up for anything.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The memoir problem</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    When you bring a recipe in from the web, what you keep is the recipe:
    ingredients, method, timing. Not the childhood anecdote, not the advert
    slots, not the newsletter box. The thing you cook from should be the
    thing you can read at a glance while something's on the hob.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why it's in a life assistant</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Because dinner is a money question, a health question and a household
    logistics question at once, and this app already holds all three. A
    recipe box that knows the budget and the allergy is a different object
    from one that doesn't.
  </p>
</section>
